    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-42885",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e84e45ca-c250-5e65-a04e-4714a485f38e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Determinant of a Triangular Matrix",
      "problem": "Consider the matrices $A$ and $B$ given below. Find $det(A)-det({B}^{T}).$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2.5 & 0.21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}|=(-1)\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 2=2 \\\\ det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2.5 & 0.21 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0.1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|=1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, $det({B}^{T})=det(B)=-6.$ \n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(A)-det({B}^{T})=2-(-6)=8.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
