    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130107",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e84e45ca-c250-5e65-a04e-4714a485f38e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Determinant of a Triangular Matrix",
      "problem": "Given that $E=5I$, where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, what is the value of $det(E)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5I & =5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}| & =5\\cdot 5\\cdot 5=125.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
