    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-69982",
      "knowledge_point_id": "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros",
      "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$Q=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the $4$th column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $4$th column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(Q) & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -4 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-1)^{1+4}|\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & =-5\\cdot (2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3)=-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(Q)=-30.\n$$",
