    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-130292",
      "knowledge_point_id": "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros",
      "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 2 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot 5\\cdot 6\\cdot (-1)=-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(B)=-60.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
