    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-158114",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped",
      "problem": "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $3$rd row of the $3\\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-2|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)^{1+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-6(4-25) \\\\  & =126.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|126|=126.\n$$",
