      "math_academy_id": "q-157695",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped",
      "problem": "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,c=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,d=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $a,$ $b,$ $c,$ and  $d:$\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M:$\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. So, we have\n\n$$\ndet(M)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]=5\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)=-30.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV=|det(M)|=|-30|=30.\n$$",
