    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-77503",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types",
      "problem": "Given that $det(A)=2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=7$\n- $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=2$\n- $\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=-\\frac{1}{2}$",
      "worked_solution": "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ndet(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have,\n\n$$\n-2\\text{det}({A}^{T})-3\\text{det}(I)=-2\\cdot 2-3\\cdot 1=-7\\ne 7.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 2=8\\ne 2.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})-\\text{det}(I)=2-1=1\\ne -\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "None"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "I only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "III only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II only"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "II and III only"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "None",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "correct_origin": "ma_successful_grade"
        }
      ],
      "difficulty": "moderate",
      "provenance": {
        "activity_question": "question-77503",
        "kp_title": "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types",
        "kp_href": null
      }
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-42876",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types",
      "problem": "Given that $A=3I$, where $I$ is the $3\\times 3$ identity matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=108$\n\nII. $\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=0$\n\nIII. $\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=54$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3I & =3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a diagonal matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}| & ={3}^{3}=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})=\\text{det}(A)=27\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n4\\text{det}({A}^{T})=4\\cdot 27=108\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\text{det}(A)-6\\,\\text{det}(I)=27-6\\cdot 1=21\\ne 0\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\text{det}({A}^{T})+\\text{det}(A)=27+27=54\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only\".",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
