  "canonical_examples": [
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10991",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e84e45ca-c250-5e65-a04e-4714a485f38e",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Determinant of a Triangular Matrix",
      "problem": "Calculate \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081590/assets/26dbd9a28532c3c262ca8015444364febabb8e269e5c6310a8fc59268f6e215c.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 10 & \\sqrt{3} & 0 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}| & =(-2)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\cdot (-2)=4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10992",
      "knowledge_point_id": "02e65c73-8c02-5403-8c5c-b1e81f81a7b6",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Determinant of a Matrix With Many Zeros",
      "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081590/assets/28a22a5f860f02e42abc699e9807de885cc01b572c23ac0d4f909fe281042e08.png)",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the $1$st column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $1$st column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 13 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 13 & 19 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot (-3)=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 2 \\cdot (-6) = -12.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10998",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3118d2af-771f-54c9-b18e-5196b87b6dca",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Volume of an N-Dimensional Parallelepiped",
      "problem": "Calculate the volume of the $4$-dimensional parallelepiped spanned by the vectors\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081590/assets/c185678d20cabf2a302c0101876f9fb67fd35cf682f547f62a9eeb99fd97946f.png)",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's construct the matrix $M,$ whose columns are the coordinates of our vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ $\\mathbf{c},$ and  $\\mathbf{d}{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14081590/assets/923b0fb4f65d4de6538658d49e32fb3256fd374b1cc94dd62256f9e966c72d24.png)\n\nThe volume of the parallelepiped spanned by these vectors equals the absolute value of the determinant of $M{:}$\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) |\n$$\n\nNotice that the $2$nd row of the determinant contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -4 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)^{2+1}|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the $2$nd row of the $3 \\times 3$ determinant contains two zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $2$nd row, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(M) & =-3|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)^{2+2}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3|\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-3(15+4) \\\\  & =-57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume is\n\n$$\nV = | \\det(M) | = |-57| = 57.\n$$",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    },
    {
      "math_academy_id": "e-10993",
      "knowledge_point_id": "a6a3d254-c96f-5aa0-9db6-a587eb7cb2de",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Expression Involving Given Determinants and Matrix Types",
      "problem": "Given that $\\det(A)=4,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\textrm{det}(A^T)=16$\n\n- $3\\textrm{det}(A^T)=12$\n\n- $\\textrm{det}(A^T)+\\textrm{det}(I)=17$",
      "worked_solution": "From the properties of determinants, we know that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A^T)=\\textrm{det}(A)=4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(I)=1.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Rather, \n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A^T)=4 \\neq 16.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n3\\textrm{det}(A^T)= 3 \\cdot 4 = 12.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A^T)+\\textrm{det}(I)= 4 + 1 = 5 \\neq 17.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
      "difficulty": null,
      "answer_fields": [],
      "missing_source_fields": [
        "difficulty",
        "answer_fields"
      ]
    }
  ]
